partialText:
&lt;p&gt;The site in this write-up is an exam-prep platform for a professional certification. It runs on WordPress with Tutor LMS for courses, WooCommerce for the shop and a card payment gateway. The learning side worked. The marketing side did not: the homepage was a stock theme, slow to change and hard to make look like a real product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We wanted a new front end, but we could not afford to break enrolments, logins or checkout while building it. This is how we did it, what went well and what hurt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The decision: hybrid, not headless
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We looked at three routes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A new WordPress theme.&lt;/strong&gt; We tried a premium LMS theme first. Its bundled plugins were old and threw fatal errors on PHP 8, so we rolled back to the old theme.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Full headless.&lt;/strong&gt; React for everything, WordPress as an API. That means rebuilding login, the student dashboard, the lesson player and checkout. For a small team that is months of work and a lot of payment risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hybrid.&lt;/strong&gt; React owns the marketing pages only. Everything that involves an account or money stays in WordPress, and React links to it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We chose the hybrid. The rule was simple: if a page needs a session or a payment, WordPress serves it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How it is wired
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. A React app that builds to two fixed files.&lt;/strong&gt; The app is React, TypeScript and Vite. We turned off code splitting and hashed filenames so the output is always &lt;code&gt;app.js&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;app.css&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;cssCodeSplit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;rollupOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;entryFileNames&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"app.js"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;assetFileNames&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"app[extname]"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;manualChunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Cloudflare Pages hosts the bundle.&lt;/strong&gt; We deploy the &lt;code&gt;dist&lt;/code&gt; folder with Wrangler. The domain's DNS did not move. Pages is only a static file host here.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm run build
npx wrangler pages deploy dist --project-name your-project --branch main
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. WordPress pages load the bundle.&lt;/strong&gt; Each marketing route (home, about, pricing, tutors, contact) is a blank WordPress page using the Elementor Canvas template, which prints no theme header or footer. The page body is three lines:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;link&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"stylesheet"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://your-project.pages.dev/app.css"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"app-root"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"module"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://your-project.pages.dev/app.js"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React Router reads the URL and renders the matching page. Because every route is also a real WordPress page, a direct visit or a refresh never returns a 404.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. React links out for everything else.&lt;/strong&gt; Register, login, dashboard, course pages and checkout are plain links to the existing WordPress URLs. The "Buy now" buttons add the WooCommerce product to the cart in the background and fall back to the classic &lt;code&gt;/?add-to-cart=ID&lt;/code&gt; link if that fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. WordPress pages get the same look.&lt;/strong&gt; A custom CSS block overrides Tutor LMS and WooCommerce colour variables and fonts. A small script, added through a snippets plugin, draws the same header and footer on WordPress pages. It checks for &lt;code&gt;#app-root&lt;/code&gt; and skips pages where React is already running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6. Launch was a one-setting change.&lt;/strong&gt; We pointed "front page" in WordPress settings at the new page and left the old homepage untouched. Rolling back meant changing that setting back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What worked well
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No migration.&lt;/strong&gt; Students, enrolments, orders and course content never moved. Nobody had to reset a password.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fast design changes.&lt;/strong&gt; A homepage change is a build and a deploy that takes under a minute. No theme editor, no page builder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cheap rollback.&lt;/strong&gt; Old homepage kept, new one switched on by a setting. Cloudflare Pages also keeps every deployment, so a bad build can be reverted from the dashboard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Payment risk stayed at zero.&lt;/strong&gt; We did not touch checkout, so we had nothing to retest there.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Free hosting for the front end.&lt;/strong&gt; The static bundle sits on the Pages free tier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Bottlenecks we hit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prices lived in two places.&lt;/strong&gt; The marketing pages had course and bundle prices typed into a data file. Months later the team changed prices in WooCommerce. Checkout charged the new prices while the homepage still advertised the old ones, at half the real amount in some cases. We only noticed when someone asked for a price change that had already been made. Anything the shop owns should be fetched from the shop, or at least checked by a script on every build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Caching hid our changes.&lt;/strong&gt; The site uses a page cache plugin. Edits to the WordPress pages or the custom CSS did not show until we purged the cache, and we lost time more than once debugging a change that was already correct. The bundle itself is safer: Pages serves it with &lt;code&gt;must-revalidate&lt;/code&gt;, so fixed filenames did not cause stale JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;We lost track of the deploy account.&lt;/strong&gt; The Pages project had been created under a different Cloudflare login from the one on the developer's machine. Wrangler also caches an account ID locally, so even after logging in with the right account it kept calling the wrong one until we set &lt;code&gt;CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID&lt;/code&gt;. Write down which account owns the project on day one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Two design systems.&lt;/strong&gt; The React pages and the WordPress pages are styled separately. Every brand change has to be made twice, and plugin updates can undo CSS overrides. Course and checkout pages still look slightly different from the marketing pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SEO needs extra work.&lt;/strong&gt; The marketing content is rendered in the browser. The WordPress page that carries it has an almost empty body, so titles and meta descriptions have to be set per page in WordPress, and we have not finished that. If organic search is your main channel, prerender the pages at build time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The homepage now depends on a second host.&lt;/strong&gt; If the Pages URL is unreachable, the homepage is blank. It has not happened to us, but it is a dependency that did not exist before.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It does not fix WordPress.&lt;/strong&gt; The plugin count, the update backlog and the bot signups on the registration form are all still there. A new front end changes how the site looks, not how much there is to maintain behind it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What we would tell another team
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Draw the line at sessions and money. Rebuild what is public and static. Leave the rest alone until you are ready to replace it properly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use fixed output filenames and one mount element. It keeps the WordPress side to three lines of HTML that never change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep the old homepage and switch with a setting. Launch and rollback become the same small action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Give every fact one owner. Prices, course lists and tutor names should come from WordPress through its REST API, not from a file in the React repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Document the deploy: which account, which project, which branch, which command.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plan the cache purge into every release checklist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treat the hybrid as a stage, not the end state. It bought us a modern front end in weeks. It also made clear which parts of WordPress we still want to replace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Would we do it again?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, for the same situation: a working WordPress back end, a weak front end, a small team and no room for downtime. If we were starting again we would fetch prices from WooCommerce from the first day and prerender the pages for search engines. Those two changes would have removed our two most visible problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building an AI Agent or LLM SaaS from scratch usually means spending the first 4–6 weeks wiring up the same boilerplate: authentication, multi-tenant workspaces, encrypted API key storage, a streaming canvas, and a visual workflow builder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To skip that repetitive setup, I built &lt;strong&gt;NexusAI&lt;/strong&gt; — a production-ready &lt;strong&gt;React 19 + TypeScript + Express + Prisma&lt;/strong&gt; starter kit designed specifically for AI agent platforms and workflow automation tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ✨ Key Architecture &amp;amp; Features
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Visual DAG Workflow Builder:&lt;/strong&gt; Interactive node-based flow editor with conditional branching (&lt;code&gt;True / False&lt;/code&gt; routing), real-time step execution tracking, and run history.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Split-Screen AI Canvas:&lt;/strong&gt; Live code/component generation alongside an interactive preview pane with version history (&lt;code&gt;v1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;v2&lt;/code&gt;, etc.) and file export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3-Tier AI Execution Engine:&lt;/strong&gt; Out-of-the-box support for &lt;strong&gt;Google Gemini&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;OpenAI&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Anthropic Claude&lt;/strong&gt; — plus an automatic &lt;strong&gt;Simulated Fallback Mode&lt;/strong&gt; so every workflow runs immediately without needing external API keys during local testing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Enterprise Security &amp;amp; Multi-Tenancy:&lt;/strong&gt; JWT cookie authentication, RBAC workspace permissions, audit logs, and &lt;strong&gt;AES-256-GCM&lt;/strong&gt; encrypted provider key storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-Config Database:&lt;/strong&gt; Ships with &lt;strong&gt;SQLite&lt;/strong&gt; + Prisma seeding for instant &lt;code&gt;npm run dev&lt;/code&gt; setup, and switches cleanly to &lt;strong&gt;PostgreSQL&lt;/strong&gt; for production.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  🚀 Try the Live Interactive Demo
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can explore the landing page, switch between all 3 live themes (Dark, Light, Oceanic Blue), and test the interactive workspace directly in your browser:&lt;br&gt;
👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://nexus-ai-template-sigma.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Live Interactive Demo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  📦 Get the Source Code
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Available as both a lightweight &lt;strong&gt;Frontend UI Kit&lt;/strong&gt; and a complete &lt;strong&gt;Full-Stack Starter Kit&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://osstudio.gumroad.com/l/nexusai-template" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Explore NexusAI Starter Kit on Gumroad&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love to hear your feedback on the architecture or any features you'd like to see added in v2!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un formulario de registro puede estar bien diseñado y aun así sentirse roto cuando llega el mensaje de validación. El campo crece, el botón baja y la persona pierde el lugar donde estaba leyendo. En móvil, ese salto es más molesto todavía.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La solución no es esconder el error ni mostrarlo en un toast que desaparece. Es reservar un espacio estable para el resultado, anunciar el cambio a tecnologías de asistencia y mantener la interacción predecible. En React, este patrón también hace más sencillas las pruebas de interfaz con email.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  El problema: el mensaje llega y mueve toda la pantalla
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagina un formulario que pide una dirección de correo temporal para crear una cuenta de prueba. Al enviar, la aplicación espera la respuesta del servidor y luego agrega un texto bajo el campo:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"field-error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Visualmente parece practico, pero el párrafo no existía durante el primer render. Cuando aparece, empuja los controles siguientes. Si la persona estaba a punto de pulsar el botón, puede terminar pulsando otra cosa.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hay dos problemas distintos:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Estabilidad visual:&lt;/strong&gt; el contenido nuevo cambia la posición de elementos existentes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Comunicación:&lt;/strong&gt; una persona que usa un lector de pantalla puede no enterarse de que la validación terminó.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El segundo problema no se arregla solo con color rojo. El estado debe tener nombre, relación con el campo y una forma clara de anunciarse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reserva el espacio antes de conocer el resultado
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Una forma sencilla es mantener el contenedor del mensaje en el DOM y darle una altura mínima. El contenido puede estar vacío cuando no hay error, pero el campo conserva su geometría.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.field-message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;min-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.5rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;margin-block-start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.35rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mh"&gt;#8a1c1c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;font-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.9rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.field-message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;empty&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;visibility&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;visibility: hidden&lt;/code&gt; conserva el espacio sin dejar un mensaje invisible en el recorrido visual. No uses &lt;code&gt;display: none&lt;/code&gt; para el estado vacío si esa decisión hace que el layout vuelva a saltar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En diseños con mensajes de dos líneas, reserva la altura realista para el caso más largo. No hace falta dejar un bloque enorme: basta con que la interfaz no cambie de forma cada vez que una respuesta del servidor es un poco mas larga.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Un componente React con estados previsibles
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El componente puede asociar el mensaje con el campo mediante &lt;code&gt;aria-describedby&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;role="alert"&lt;/code&gt; es apropiado para un error que necesita atención inmediata, pero no conviene aplicarlo a cada pequeño cambio de ayuda mientras la persona escribe.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailField&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messageId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email-message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"field"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;htmlFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Correo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;electr&lt;/span&gt;ó&lt;span class="n"&gt;nico&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;invalid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;describedby&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;messageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;messageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"field-message"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"alert"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Hay una frontera importante: el cliente no debe inventar que un email fue entregado. Puede mostrar “comprobando” mientras espera y “no pudimos validar todavía” cuando la respuesta falla. Los textos deben describir el estado real, no el estado que nos gustaria tener.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para una explicación relacionada, vale la pena revisar estos &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/react-estados-de-verificacion-que-si-explican-43j9"&gt;estados de verificación que sí explican&lt;/a&gt;. El mismo principio funciona para errores de formato, códigos expirados y respuestas tardías.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  CSS para accesibilidad y rendimiento
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reservar espacio evita cambios de layout, pero no convierte automáticamente el formulario en accesible. Revisa también el contraste, el foco visible y el orden de lectura. El mensaje debe aparecer cerca del campo que lo origina, no en una zona que la persona tiene que buscar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Si prefieres animar el estado, anima &lt;code&gt;opacity&lt;/code&gt; o &lt;code&gt;transform&lt;/code&gt; con cuidado y respeta &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;. No animes la altura de todo el formulario cada vez que llega una respuesta. Ese movimiento puede ser costoso y además dificulta leer el mensaje.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;no-preference&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nc"&gt;.field-message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;transition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;160ms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nc"&gt;.field-message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;transition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;El rendimiento percibido mejora cuando el botón no cambia de posición, aunque la petición tarde unos milisegundos más de lo esperado. Es un detalle pequeño que suele tener mas impacto que añadir otro indicador decorativo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cómo probarlo con mensajes de email
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En una prueba de componente, comprueba primero la geometría conceptual: el mensaje existe aunque esté vacío, tiene una relación accesible con el input y cambia cuando llega el resultado. En una prueba de extremo a extremo, provoca tanto un email inválido como una respuesta retrasada.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para una revisión manual puedes usar una bandeja aislada como &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tempmailso&lt;/a&gt;, pero no mezcles esa comprobación con cuentas personales ni con datos reales. También ayuda comparar las &lt;a href="https://dev.to/hannahdev56/saas-emails-de-trial-con-senales-utiles-48i5"&gt;señales útiles en emails de trial&lt;/a&gt; con los estados que realmente enseña tu interfaz.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Si el equipo escribe &lt;code&gt;tempail mail&lt;/code&gt; en una incidencia, trátalo como texto de búsqueda y no como nombre de una variable o un contrato de API. Los typos aparecen en el trabajo real; los identificadores del código deben permanecer estables.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist y preguntas rápidas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Antes de cerrar el formulario, revisa:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El mensaje tiene espacio reservado desde el primer render?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El input usa &lt;code&gt;aria-invalid&lt;/code&gt; cuando corresponde?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿&lt;code&gt;aria-describedby&lt;/code&gt; apunta siempre a un nodo existente?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El foco y el contraste siguen siendo visibles con error?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La prueba cubre respuesta lenta, error y reintento?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La interfaz respeta &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;¿Debo reservar espacio para todos los mensajes?&lt;/strong&gt; Para errores, ayuda contextual y estados que cambian la posición de los controles, normalmente sí. Si el mensaje aparece fuera del flujo, como una notificación global, usa otro patrón y asegúrate de anunciarlo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;¿Puedo validar solo al perder el foco?&lt;/strong&gt; Sí, suele ser menos ruidoso que validar cada tecla. Para el envío final, muestra el error junto al campo y conserva el espacio para que el resultado no desplace el botón.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;¿Qué pasa si el mensaje es muy largo?&lt;/strong&gt; Define una longitud razonable, escribe el texto con claridad y deja que crezca cuando sea necesario. Un salto ocasional es preferible a cortar información importante, pero los casos normales deben quedar estables.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un formulario React no tiene que elegir entre buen aspecto y buena accesibilidad. Si reserva espacio, comunica el estado y prueba la llegada real de los mensajes, la validación se siente tranquila incluso cuando el email tarda. Esa combinación de CSS sencillo y estados honestos suele ser el pulido que más se nota.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Weigh is a small web app for one job: helping you choose between two or more options (two job offers, staying or moving, and so on). You rate each option on the same 7 questions, and it shows a weighted score. There is no account, no server and no AI. Decisions live in &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feature I wanted most was &lt;strong&gt;Ask a friend&lt;/strong&gt;: a friend rates the same options &lt;em&gt;blind&lt;/em&gt;, then you see where you agree and where you differ. Usually that needs a database. Here is how it works with nothing but links.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The round trip
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You finish a decision and tap &lt;strong&gt;Ask a friend&lt;/strong&gt;. The app builds a link that contains the decision title and the option names, but &lt;strong&gt;not your ratings&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your friend opens it, answers the same 7 questions, and gets their own result.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Their result screen gives them a link back to you. That link contains their answers. You open it and see "you vs friend": whether the winner matches and which question you disagree on most.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing is stored anywhere except the two browsers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Encoding state into the URL
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The payload is JSON, UTF-8 encoded, then base64, then URL-encoded:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeBase64&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;obj&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;obj&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bytes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TextEncoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;binary&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fromCharCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;btoa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;binary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;TextEncoder&lt;/code&gt; matters: plain &lt;code&gt;btoa&lt;/code&gt; throws on non-Latin-1 characters, and people name their options in every language.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Making the links ~5x shorter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first version put the whole decision in the link, including the full text of all 7 questions. That made links long enough to look like spam in WhatsApp.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix was to send only what the receiver can't rebuild. The questions are fixed templates in the app, so a link carries a &lt;code&gt;templateId&lt;/code&gt; and the receiver looks the text up locally:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FriendV2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;templateId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// no ratings&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The version field keeps old links working: if &lt;code&gt;v&lt;/code&gt; isn't 2, the app decodes the old full format.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the friend's answers come back, the app checks whether this browser holds a decision with the same option ids. If it does, you are the owner and you get the comparison. If not, it's the friend's own device and they just see their result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Scoring
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The score is a plain weighted average on a 1-5 scale. When two options tie exactly, the one that wins on the highest-weighted question goes first. That uses the user's own priorities instead of a random pick:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;decision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;criteria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;option&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;answers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;weight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;totalWeight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  SEO for a client-side app
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An SPA that renders in the browser is nearly invisible to crawlers. I didn't want SSR for a tiny app, so:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;guide pages ("how to choose between two job offers" and similar) are generated as static HTML at build time. They import the &lt;strong&gt;same&lt;/strong&gt; scoring code as the app, so the worked examples on the page always match what the app would compute;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;each guide's button opens the app with the example pre-filled (&lt;code&gt;/new?title=...&amp;amp;o=...&amp;amp;o=...&lt;/code&gt;);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the home page ships a small static block inside &lt;code&gt;#root&lt;/code&gt; that React replaces on load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why no AI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A chatbot can give you a confident answer about your life from one paragraph of context. I wanted the opposite: the answer comes from your own honest ratings, and you can see exactly why an option won. The questions are inspired by Schwartz's research on basic human values and by regret minimization. The weights are the app's defaults and I'm open about that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can try it here: &lt;a href="https://weigh-pwa.web.app/?ref=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://weigh-pwa.web.app/?ref=devto&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love feedback on two things. Did the result match your gut feeling? And which of the 7 questions felt wrong for your decision?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This post was written with AI assistance and reviewed against the app's source code.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  What's New in React 19
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React 19 introduces several significant updates, including stable Server Components, automatic memoization via the React Compiler, and streamlined form handling. Here are the key features you need to know:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  React Compiler
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The React Compiler automatically optimizes and memoizes components during build time, eliminating the need for manual optimization using &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;. This feature simplifies component development and reduces the need for boilerplate code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Stable Server Components &amp;amp; Actions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server Components allow components to run on the server and interact with databases directly, reducing the amount of JavaScript sent to the user's browser. This feature enables faster and more efficient server-side rendering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Simplified Form Handling
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Built-in action props on forms and new hooks (useActionState, useOptimistic, and useFormStatus) eliminate repetitive boilerplate for loading states and submissions. This feature streamlines form development and reduces the need for manual state management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Open Governance
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is now managed under the independent React Foundation (part of the Linux Foundation), rather than being controlled solely by Meta. This change reflects the growing community-driven nature of React development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Pros of Using React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React offers several benefits for developers, including:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Reusable component architecture that enables easy maintenance and scaling of complex user interfaces&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Access to a massive ecosystem and community of developers, with thousands of third-party libraries and tools available&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Strong performance features like the Virtual DOM, concurrent rendering, and server-side execution&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Cross-platform potential, with knowledge of React transferring directly to mobile app development using React Native&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cons of Using React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While React is a powerful tool, it also has some drawbacks to consider:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;React is not a complete framework, requiring the use of external tools for routing, global state, and authentication&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;A steep learning curve, with challenges like JSX syntax, complex hook lifecycles, and deciding between client and server boundaries&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;A rapid pace of change, with frequent updates and architectural shifts requiring developers to constantly relearn best practices&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Configuration overhead, with significant boilerplate required for large-scale production apps unless using an opinionated meta-framework&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Evaluating React for Your Project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're evaluating React for a specific project, consider the following factors:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Project type (e.g., static website, interactive dashboard, full-stack app)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Your team's experience level with JavaScript/TypeScript&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/presage-2026-11-04"&gt;Presage SmartSpectra SDK Challenge&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Blink_of_an_Eye_%28book%29" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;In the Blink of an Eye&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, film editor &lt;a href="https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0004555/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Walter Murch&lt;/a&gt; asks why humans blink thousands of times a day when our eyes only need moisture a fraction as often. His answer is that a blink is a mental punctuation mark—the physical hinge where one thought cuts to the next, paced by our breathing. &lt;a href="https://www.imdb.com/title/tt15575678/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Before Your Eyes&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; turned that reflex into loss, cutting away every scene the instant an involuntary blink landed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to build a slower darkroom where &lt;strong&gt;you steer a five-scene memory essay with your eyes and chest—no keyboard—using one strict rule:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A blink is a cut, not a click.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A naive blink detector ruins reading because your eyelids twitch every few seconds while you are still halfway through a sentence. So &lt;strong&gt;AFTERIMAGE&lt;/strong&gt; locks every beat for &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;4.2 s&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;Shutter Armed&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt; on the &lt;strong&gt;Presage SmartSpectra SDK (&lt;code&gt;@smartspectra/node-sdk@3.5.0&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt; before a quick blink can turn the page. Across my &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;352.4 s&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; live narrated sitting (&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;670&lt;/code&gt; keyframes&lt;/strong&gt; in &lt;code&gt;sample-trace.json&lt;/code&gt;), I blinked &lt;strong&gt;64 times&lt;/strong&gt;: the dwell gate rejected &lt;strong&gt;30&lt;/strong&gt; early reading blinks (rippling the aperture silently instead of skipping prose) and let the other &lt;strong&gt;34&lt;/strong&gt; cut cleanly whenever a page was armed (&lt;code&gt;15&lt;/code&gt; deliberate cuts completed the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;239.2 s&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; essay).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Between cuts, three involuntary signals run the room:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Live SmartSpectra Signal&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What Raw SDK Emits (&lt;code&gt;30 Hz&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How AFTERIMAGE Processes It&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Effect on Screen &amp;amp; Sound&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quick Blink Cut&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;face.landmarks&lt;/code&gt; (478 px pts) + &lt;code&gt;face.blinking&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Scale-invariant 6-pt EAR (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt; 0.21&lt;/code&gt;) fired &lt;strong&gt;on reopen&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt; 650 ms&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;360 ms&lt;/code&gt; debounce) with a &lt;code&gt;0.25&lt;/code&gt; EAR native-descent guard + &lt;code&gt;4.2 s&lt;/code&gt; dwell&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Aperture click sound + cut across 15 beats (early blinks ripple instead of advancing)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Long Eye-Close Pause&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sustained low EAR (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt; 0.21&lt;/code&gt; held &lt;code&gt;≥ 1.1 s&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Descent guard (&lt;code&gt;lastEar &amp;gt;= 0.25&lt;/code&gt;) + &lt;code&gt;≥ 400 ms&lt;/code&gt; suppression stops native &lt;code&gt;face.blinking&lt;/code&gt; pulses during long closures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pauses / resumes sitting cleanly (&lt;code&gt;4&lt;/code&gt; live pauses verified without skipping beats)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Horizontal Gaze (&lt;code&gt;L / C / R&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Iris centers &lt;code&gt;468&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;473&lt;/code&gt; + nose/cheek yaw&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Additive deviation (&lt;code&gt;0.5 + irisDelta * 0.95 + yawDelta * 0.55&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt; 0.455 / &amp;gt; 0.545&lt;/code&gt; + hysteresis&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Smooth crossfade, sharper triptych panel, caption swap, stereo WebAudio pan&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chest Breath &amp;amp; Phase&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;breathing.upperTrace&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;breathing.rate&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;face.talking&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;4.5 s&lt;/code&gt; OLS linear detrend + 2nd-diff curvature steadiness + &lt;code&gt;≥ 5&lt;/code&gt;-frame talking guard (&lt;code&gt;807/807&lt;/code&gt; checks live)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Clears SVG blur (&lt;code&gt;feGaussianBlur&lt;/code&gt;), slows drift, swells pink-noise bed; &lt;code&gt;≥ 60%&lt;/code&gt; chimes &amp;amp; reveals hidden note&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tonal Echo&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;face.expression&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;HAPPY&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;SAD&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;FEAR&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ANGRY&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;NEUTRAL&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;0.82/0.18&lt;/code&gt; warm (&lt;code&gt;≥ 45%&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;code&gt;0.90/0.10&lt;/code&gt; heavy (&lt;code&gt;≥ 86%&lt;/code&gt;) EMA for downward laptop camera angles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Subtle light warmth &amp;amp; ambient chord detune (&lt;code&gt;neutral&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;warm&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;heavy&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;478-Point Silhouette&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Raw &lt;code&gt;face.landmarks&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;[316..564, 10..350]&lt;/code&gt; px)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Normalized to &lt;code&gt;[0..1]&lt;/code&gt; oval/eye geometry on the Node bridge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vector rangefinder reticle on &lt;code&gt;/calibrate&lt;/code&gt; (zero camera pixels in browser DOM)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What a Plate Actually Reads Like
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On &lt;strong&gt;Plate I (&lt;em&gt;Morning Steam · Kitchen table · 06:14 AM&lt;/em&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt;, opening Beat 1 puts an untouched kitchen table in front of you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;First light slips between the blind slats onto an empty kitchen table. Nobody has spoken yet; the kettle only stopped whistling a minute ago.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shifting your eyes Left, Center, or Right swaps which detail sharpens and which caption speaks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Left — Venetian Blinds:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;"Dust rises slowly through four narrow bands of early light."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Center — Ceramic Cup:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;"Steam still curls off the dark surface untouched since six."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Right — Folded Note:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;"Half a sentence pressed into napkin paper beside the spoon."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Settle your chest breath past &lt;code&gt;60%&lt;/code&gt; steadiness and a two-note chime wakes a hidden cobalt line underneath:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Marginalia developed: “You meant to finish packing last night, then stood here twenty minutes just listening to the refrigerator hum.”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That breath lock never fakes itself. Across my silent &lt;strong&gt;Sitting 2 (&lt;code&gt;205.5 s&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Plates III and IV&lt;/strong&gt; dropped in steadiness and honestly unlocked only &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;1/3&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; notes (&lt;code&gt;11/15&lt;/code&gt; total). Across my spoken &lt;strong&gt;Sitting 3 (&lt;code&gt;239.2 s&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt;, talking while narrating held the freeze cleanly (&lt;code&gt;72%&lt;/code&gt; average clarity, &lt;code&gt;15/15&lt;/code&gt; notes unlocked), while in an earlier dark-theme rehearsal (&lt;strong&gt;Solar Coral&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;code&gt;194.7 s&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;57%&lt;/code&gt; clarity) losing the chest trace on &lt;strong&gt;Plates II–IV&lt;/strong&gt; drew flat dashed lines (&lt;code&gt;0/3&lt;/code&gt; notes) instead of guessing a wave.&lt;/p&gt;




